+### Speed wave 3: S3-8, the library lobby, the jukebox, the studio STAGE tab and the level select (2026-11-15)
+
+Speed audit 3's S3-8 (S2-4, family 5). The audit's numbers (2026-11-12) were re-measured on the tip this package started
+from (`d198b77`, libmgba, `make -C gba test-speed2 SPEED2="library studio action"`): the lobby 37.4 ms (23.9 fps, 53 frames
+of 50 ms in 103), the jukebox 41.0 / 42.0 playing (20.0 / 19.8), the studio STAGE tab 38.0 (21.7), the level select 35.8
+(24.6) and its slide 49.7 (17.8), the Almanac list move 33.9 (26.6). Where the time went (windows with a part left out):
+the lobby drew its wall, 4 mini shelves of ~40 spines, eight books (~25 calls each), the desk and two windows' worth of
+text on every frame; the jukebox's **record alone was 11 ms** (14 circles a frame, `ellipse_draw`), its list 9, its
+chrome 6.7 (the backdrop's dots and hearts drawn under both windows); the STAGE tab's title 4.6, bulbs 4.2, chibis 7.2;
+the level select's outlined text 9.7 (title 6.4, blurb 3.3).
+
+**Exact steps (frames identical).**
+
+* **The lobby is a kept picture and a retained stage** (`lib_common.c`; the family `DOOMO_BACKDROP_LIBRARY`, the second
+  picture user after the kitchen's S2-4c: `backdrop.h`). One baked picture (key 0, 38 KB of ROM) holds the wall, the
+  mini shelves, the display shelf's plank, the desk, the window's frame and day sky and the speech bubble's window (its
+  glint left out: `ui_glint_hold`). Everything that moves is a layer of SP7's stage, in the order the old drawing painted
+  them (`LL_*`, 23 layers: the window's cloud, mullions and highlight, **at night the whole window over the day one**; the
+  title and the badge; the eight books; the skull; the candle; Quillby with the desk's rects put back over his feet
+  and the desk's name; the bubble's first line **with its glint** (`ui_window_glint_draw`, a new helper that
+  `ui_window` itself now calls); its text; its progress bar; five dust motes; the hint pill, a layer of its own: drawn after the stage it made one dirt box with a toast over the whole screen). A layer's box holds every pixel it paints,
+  its key everything they depend on (the language, the glyph page and the options first: `ui_panel_key`). Two things
+  the boxes taught: boxes that **touch** are merged by the stage (a header strip and a text block directly under it
+  became one big dirty box until a row was left between them), and a book's box must stay narrower than its 26-px pitch
+  (the picked book's sparkles are wider, so its neighbours redraw while it bobs). The mini shelves are in the picture; the
+  one place the old drawing painted them over something is a cloud that leaves the window, and then the window's layer
+  grows to the shelf and draws it again. The stage's 1.3 KB of memory is the lobby's scene scratch (`LOB_TAG`; a book
+  opened on top takes the scratch and the lobby binds it again when back). 320x240 (not baked, the GBA never runs it)
+  and the PS1 (`#if`ed out) draw the old order; the Dreamcast takes the old order too (no kept pictures).
+* **The jukebox's record is stamps** (`lib_jukebox.c`). The disc (rim, grooves, label, spindle) does not turn, only the
+  glints do: one stamp of the family per label colour (32) and two for the tonearm (idle, playing), baked from the very
+  code that draws them (`disc_body`, `tonearm`; `plat_stage_stamp`), ~3 KB each: 11 ms of circles became ~1.5 ms of
+  blits. The glints (three rects) stay live. The backdrop is drawn with the two windows as holes
+  (`lib_chrome_holes`, `ui_backdrop_holes`: nothing lies wholly under an opaque window, 2 ms); the Almanac's list and
+  preview windows get the same (`lib_almanac.c`).
+* **The STAGE tab's title is a solid retained panel** (`studio_stage.c` `poster_title`, `PANEL_OW_OBJECTIVE`'s entry), and
+  the two rows of marquee bulbs are stamps of the library family (4: top / bottom, two phases; baked on the tab's own
+  rect): the bulbs' 56 circles became two blits.
+
+**Pixel-changing (GBA and the native build; the PS1 and the Dreamcast draw as before: `LS_KEEP`)** (SPEED DEFAULTS, owner
+decision "VISUAL TRADE-OFFS APPROVED": the level select's text as kept panels and the slide shows the old preview):
+
+* **Level select, title and blurb + numbers' card as solid panels** (`levels_select.c`, cross-stream: the LEVELS stream's
+  file; the commit is marked `cross-stream:`). A solid panel keeps its whole rect, backdrop included, and the platform's panel
+  rule wants a body under text, so the panel draws the backdrop's bands and seams in its rect (`ui_backdrop_rows`: the same
+  pixels as `ui_backdrop_flat`) before the text: **the backdrop's drifting dots, hearts and twinkles do not show in
+  those two rects** (the title's text box, and the blurb with the card down to its boss line). The pips are drawn after the
+  title (the picked pip's shadow lies over the title's outline for a pixel or two at the lowest of its bob). The blurb and
+  the card are one panel because the card lies over a third blurb line.
+* **Level select, the slide keeps the old picture**: while the slide runs (about 10 ticks) the old level's held 3D picture
+  stays where it was (`plat_view_hold(PLAT_VIEW_PUT, ls.pv_key)`); the new level is loaded and rendered once the slide has
+  ended. The slide's motion is gone (the picture snaps), its eight renders a level change (10 ms of view and 8 ms of copy
+  each) are one. The hold key lives in the struct's own padding: `ls.pv_held` is `pv_key != 0` and `flash_t`, `msg_t`
+  are bytes (EWRAM +0 B).
+
+Before / after screenshots (level select at rest and in the slide, the title and blurb rects; every other screen is
+frame-identical, below): `evidence/S3-8/shots/before` and `shots/after` of the speed2 scratch folder (not in the repo).
+
+**Same pixels (the exact steps).** `test_lib_kept` plays each screen twice, with the stage and the stamps off and on, and
+compares every frame: the lobby (the cursor along the shelf and back, a locked book poked, a book opened and closed, day
